A boot of the persistent runtime asked for while another is under way waits
for that one rather than failing.

Two things boot it on iOS: the splash, when the app is opened, and a wake for
background refresh that finds the app launched with no window. When the
operator opens the app while a wake is still booting, the second call found
the worker thread alive and returned -3, and the app fell back to its classic
mode for the rest of that launch. The call now claims the boot under the gate's
own lock, so only one caller ever starts the worker, and a caller that finds a
boot under way waits for it on the gate and returns what it came to.

--- a/vendor/nativephp/mobile/resources/xcode/Include/Bridge/PHP.c
+++ b/vendor/nativephp/mobile/resources/xcode/Include/Bridge/PHP.c
@@ -622,2 +622,15 @@
 int persistent_php_boot(const char *bootstrapPath) {
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_persistent_boot_mutex);
+    int under_way = g_persistent_boot_state == PERSISTENT_BOOT_IN_PROGRESS;
+    if (!under_way && !persistent_initialized && !worker_thread_alive) {
+        g_persistent_boot_state = PERSISTENT_BOOT_IN_PROGRESS;
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_persistent_boot_mutex);
+
+    if (under_way) {
+        fprintf(stderr, "persistent_php_boot: a boot is under way, waiting for it\n");
+        fflush(stderr);
+        return wait_for_persistent_boot(60) == 0 ? 0 : -3;
+    }
+
     if (persistent_initialized) {
